{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310655,"nonce":"StmT4MFeziDDcFir/jzTEZnC2dg=","title":"FIXED (46ccca49): `continue` in an array `for (x in arr)` infinite-looped because the desugar put _i++ as the last BODY statement of a ...","message":"`for (x in arr) { ...; continue; }` infinitely looped (index never advanced).\nRoot cause: `ast_lowering.rs` desugared the array for-in to\n`{ var _i=0; var _len=arr.length; while (_i\u003c_len) { var x=arr[_i]; body; _i++; } }`\nwith **`_i++` as the last statement of the while BODY**. A `continue` in the body\nbranched to the condition and skipped `_i++`. (MIR: the index phi's continue\nback-edge carried the pre-increment value; increment sat in the latch block.)\nThe range-for desugar right above already used `TypedStatement::For { update: i++ }`\nprecisely so continue runs the update — the array path just didn't.\n**Fix:** desugar the array for-in the same way — `TypedStatement::For` with `_i++`\nas the `update` and a body of `{ var x=arr[_i]; body }` (ast_lowering.rs ~12156).\nWas LATENT since the for-in desugar was written (git blame e043f520), NOT a recent\nregression; both cranelift and --llvm failed identically → shared HIR→MIR, not a\nbackend opt. Verified: continue / break / both / nested / range-for / plain sum all\ncorrect; BERT encoder cosine unchanged (0.99999).\n\n**Debugging technique that cracked it** ([[feedback_verify_codegen_by_disasm]]-adjacent):\nthe POST-merge `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR` had already inlined ArrayIterator.hasNext/next, hiding\nthe builder. Adding a PRE-merge dump (before the stdlib merge inlines) showed the loop\nwas ALREADY inline-index with an `@array_length` CALL and no hasNext/next — proving it's\na range-style desugar, not `emit_iterator_while_loop`, which led straight to\nast_lowering. Also: the BLADE cache is `.rayzor/blade/cache` RELATIVE to CWD — `rm -rf\n~/.rayzor` and `--no-cache` do NOT clear it; `rm -rf \u003ccwd\u003e/.rayzor`.\n\n`haxe_array_join` dereferenced every element as a `*HaxeString` — correct for\n`Array\u003cString\u003e`, but for `Array\u003cInt\u003e` the raw values were treated as pointers and\ncrashed. Added `haxe_array_join_typed(arr, sep, elem_tag)` (runtime/src/haxe_array.rs)\nthat converts each element via `haxe_value_to_string_by_tag` (tag 5=String fast path;\n1/2/4/6=Int/Bool/Float/Ref), routed at the call site in hir_to_mir (~9952, next to the\npush/f64 special case) with the element type tag. `HaxeArray` carries no element tag, so\nthe tag MUST come from the compiler.\n\n`if (x != Std.parseInt(s))` crashed; `var p = Std.parseInt(s); if (x != p)` was FINE, and an\ninline USER `Null\u003cInt\u003e` fn was FINE. Root cause: `Std.parseInt`/`parseFloat` return a RAW\nprimitive (i64, null = i64::MIN) despite a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` type. `lower_nullable_prim_eq`\n(hir_to_mir ~29712) classifies Optional{Int} and passes the operand to `haxe_null_int_eq`,\nwhich derefs it as a `*DynamicValue` box pointer (type_system.rs:2650) — so a raw value (42)\nbecomes address 42 → crash. Two Null\u003cprim\u003e representations coexist: boxed pointer (user fns,\nvariables) vs raw-sentinel (parseInt). `var p:Null\u003cInt\u003e = parseInt(..)` works because the\nassignment boxes via `haxe_box_int_ptr`. From MIR diff (pre-merge): hoisted did\n`call @haxe_box_int_ptr` then null_int_eq; inline did `bitcast to *u8` then null_int_eq.\n**Fix:** `box_if_raw` in lower_nullable_prim_eq — if an Optional operand lowers to a\nNON-pointer register (raw prim), box it via `haxe_box_{int,float,bool}_ptr`; already-boxed\noperands pass through. Verified inline parseInt/parseFloat in ==/!= (null parses + the\nwhile-compare loop) + no regression on boxed vars/user-fns/==null/Optional-vs-Optional. The\nBERT tokenizer harness now compares in-Haxe (32/32). See [[project_bert_second_architecture]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_native_codegen_continue_and_loop_output` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}